    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "it's not just grief though. it feels more like a math problem. i spent two decades investing every bit of energy, money, and time i had, and now the balance is zero. i'm older, i'm broke, and the foundation is gone. it feels like i've been playing a game where the rules changed halfway through so i can't even win now. how am i supposed to just \"fight\" for a future when all the capital is gone? it just feels like a massive sunk cost.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your perception of life as a math problem where the equations suddenly shift is a heavy way to carry a real crisis, not necessarily one you chose. That feeling of massive sunk cost is a common psychological trap where the past's hard gains make current losses feel like total failures, but bankruptcy does not erase your accumulated wisdom or resilience. Instead of trying to \"win a game\" with missing rules, try shifting your focus from the impossible balance you spent years building to the small, self-contained investments of care you can make for yourself today. A therapist can help you reframe these numbers as a temporary setback rather than a permanent identity so that fighting forward becomes a choice rather than an obligation.",
